#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167938} :activity/id #uuid "aee81a52-a15f-57d8-b2cd-df2429271786" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050989 :topic/id #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" :topic/math-academy-id 3198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168045 :tutorial/content "The **Fourier convergence theorem** describes how a Fourier series behaves, especially at points where the original function is not continuous.\n\nA Fourier series is built from smooth sine and cosine waves, so it cannot “jump” instantly the way a function with a jump discontinuity can. Instead, the series *converges* to an average value at these jumps.\n\nMore precisely, let $f(x)$ be a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function, and let its Fourier series be\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(\na_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThen the Fourier series converges for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. The value it converges to depends on whether $f$ is continuous at $x$.\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ the series converges to the function value:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ the series converges to the midpoint of the one-sided limits:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\dfrac{f^-(x)+f^+(x)}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $f^-(x)$ and $f^+(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of $f$ at $x,$ respectively.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d62d7d1-1939-536d-8fef-b02c4d023b32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14717 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0daa028f-4325-5685-88f2-3cbbcf622af2" :step/math-academy-id 598800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167957}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166575 :question/id #uuid "7e478876-8258-54a8-9dd7-81978b7cf9af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21865"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049995}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480684}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"} :step/id #uuid "31151663-c118-573c-a32e-a71852a7ab4b" :step/math-academy-id 598801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168046 :tutorial/content "A powerful application of Fourier series is *evaluating infinite sums*. The strategy is to find a known Fourier series that contains the sum we want, and then choose a convenient value of $x$ to plug in.\n\nFor example, consider the function $F(x)=x^2$ defined on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nLet's use this to find the value of the infinite sum $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}.$ \n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(n\\cdot0\\right) = \\cos\\left(0\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nBy the *pointwise convergence theorem*, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(0\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{12}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8db745e6-4117-50ca-a3ea-393692db7e84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14721 :tutorial/title "Using Fourier Series For Finding Infinite Sums"} :step/id #uuid "4bdf108a-d330-5888-ae50-49c4e0f66ede" :step/math-academy-id 598804 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166576 :question/id #uuid "0e966ca9-eab2-5443-9581-9b7cb8ecd4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432959}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "42cd3b2e-2abc-5095-b2fe-87d2a522be3b" :step/math-academy-id 598805 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168047 :tutorial/content "Even though the Fourier series converges to the correct value at each point (using the midpoint rule at jumps), the *graphs of the partial sums* can behave strangely near a jump discontinuity.\n\nSuppose $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=x_0$. Then:\n\n- The Fourier series converges at the jump to the midpoint:\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=\\dfrac{f^-(x_0)+f^+(x_0)}{2}.\n$$\n\n- However, for large $N$, the partial sum\n\n$$\nS_N(x)=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\left(\na_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\n\\right)\n$$\n\ntypically *overshoots* above the higher side of the jump and *undershoots* below the lower side.\n\nAs $N$ increases, the oscillations get squeezed into a smaller neighborhood around $x_0$, but the size of the overshoot does *not* go to $0$. This persistent overshoot near jump discontinuities is called the **Gibbs phenomenon**.\n\nFor example, for the Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)=x,$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ the phenomenon can be observed at each point of discontinuity on the graph below.\n\n![](images/55/55a772d1f18a80a16772e6a898917a3ad863ee33a3c5b482d9201b7d22c91089.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c33817ab-d849-5e36-8059-f0cb3e6146cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14722 :tutorial/title "Gibbs Phenomenon"} :step/id #uuid "bba9f02f-db71-5d7b-8afd-c5b08062d34c" :step/math-academy-id 598808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168048 :tutorial/content "As we saw, the partial sums $S_N(x)$ appear to converge to the function $f(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$. The *Fourier convergence theorem* specifies the exact value to which the series converges.\n\n- At any point $x_0$ where $f(x)$ is *continuous*, the Fourier series converges to the function value:\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=f(x_0)\n$$\n\n- At any point $x_0$ where $f(x)$ has a *jump discontinuity*, the Fourier series converges to the midpoint of the jump (the average of the one-sided limits):\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=\\dfrac{f^-(x_0)+f^+(x_0)}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/52/5261e83960334e21396e3c7e3e2c0af82154dba29f99a338f22d5ef615d1af80.png)\n\nFor our sawtooth function above, the discontinuities occur at $x=(2k+1)\\pi$. At $x=\\pi$, for example, the series converges to:\n\n$$\nS(\\pi)=\\dfrac{f^-(\\pi)+f^+(\\pi)}{2} = \\dfrac{\\pi + (-\\pi)}{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of the limit function $S(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5731fc49b0bca19ae60b9a5bc14eae5ee64f8ff36ebae85dcf2ffe0a4270708.png)\n\n**Note:** The limit function $S(x)$ is the same as $f(x)$ except at the jump discontinuities. If $f(x)$ were continuous everywhere, then its Fourier series would converge to $f(x)$ at every point, and we could write $f(x)=S(x)$ instead of $f(x)\\sim S(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "73760171-1fe4-5fe1-ae35-b5efeeb39cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14761 :tutorial/title "An Example of Convergence"} :step/id #uuid "18f33be1-5014-56eb-a799-174a9420a064" :step/math-academy-id 601089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168054 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $F(x)=x$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of its $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim S(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin\\left(nx\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/52/5261e83960334e21396e3c7e3e2c0af82154dba29f99a338f22d5ef615d1af80.png)\n\nWe can approximate the function $f(x)$ by summing a finite number of terms from its Fourier series. These approximations are called *partial sums*:\n\n$$\nS_N(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\dfrac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin\\left(nx\\right)\n$$\n\nAs $N$ increases, the partial sums match the graph of $f(x)$ more closely.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3020fcc3aa06bec65384bb3ba852b289fd1d705e554d39cc3be9b41db6623d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcb1351c-d635-5660-9fec-0f4c5c5f9018" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14872 :tutorial/title "An Example of Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "48047656-abbd-570b-8b4c-235ee9655e83" :step/math-academy-id 607545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167950}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166993}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481706} :activity/id #uuid "3a312771-af16-5ebc-966b-7a48dcb2b02c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050989 :topic/id #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" :topic/math-academy-id 3198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "857250c9-a9b1-5080-b2c5-1728296672ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480334} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e653195-c3d5-582e-822a-b0c443f4aade" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac1dfb2b-dc39-5a8f-bb78-6916e280fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343361" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ x+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=4 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-3)+{f}^{+}(-3)}{2}=\\frac{4+(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "00120d36-1a04-5461-a0b6-aa8a3c9fb95d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481688}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3303fe09-6ad0-505a-89d8-a19574971d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315415} {:answer/id #uuid "69514901-5c64-5d07-b95a-d45b7fa88118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315712} {:answer/id #uuid "a1035538-7e14-55b0-ac2d-8d7e5266d7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316026} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b0e268d-0a7c-5c87-942a-0d527b2e7230" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "526509c1-9276-5c3d-8d48-c9645209f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315588} {:answer/id #uuid "55ea1062-c9e1-5062-8d07-1555f9a98009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315611} {:answer/id #uuid "b78b7f35-d5d7-5ae4-be39-18600507be7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316159} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0c938c-160a-52cd-8dd0-6140a987639e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315588} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f1fe62-5fe6-5d2f-a137-b4f3a4585631" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316633} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b65ed1d-642e-56f2-bfbe-4ef0a45f2f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315459} {:answer/id #uuid "b855acb5-e97c-5841-8d72-85aa1b7718a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315459} :answer-field/id #uuid "32132a2d-5a62-506e-a3d9-84e46c8a7381" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c35aee6-7694-50b8-99ff-38af1b21180b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315189} {:answer/id #uuid "13531dbc-f785-5e13-9d29-9d280d8db742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315233} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9a9a9c-8530-5ab3-8a17-1e2278aba947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315567} {:answer/id #uuid "9666b55d-0e86-50aa-b086-53a192b7da15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315567} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f23a1a7-b2fc-50dd-b7d3-dea39ae4eda7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b3adfef-3238-5b0a-8f95-7aed0a3d3235" :question/math-academy-id "q-343443" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -5, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ 3 & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2b1a2a91-c2f9-5403-91c4-ab519a9b3f36"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480300} :answer-field/id #uuid "539ad74a-5113-59bb-99ce-f5b195bb6b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "967d4b0f-c5b8-576b-b414-bb183f8a1110" :question/math-academy-id "q-343368" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "344150df-b5f5-5759-8286-dbd7bc237cde" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39262ec1-dc62-5850-81ab-fe6d8919dd1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375039} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d599d3-7bd1-53bd-bb88-263de9528713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375040} {:answer/id #uuid "b131c64a-a172-53be-b5f5-bc1db39b9a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375041} {:answer/id #uuid "d26f404d-eaf4-50ca-a206-7c14a23985d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375042}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375040} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf7482ba-c42e-5af4-af91-a8b902345ce3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375038} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2e69db1-8297-5643-a6d7-62dd5d120cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375044} {:answer/id #uuid "12391f9d-def3-5a6b-8590-2ef2dbae1fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375045} {:answer/id #uuid "b19804c1-d1d5-5877-8aed-fa5c1a2b5d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375046} :answer-field/id #uuid "99d5ada7-8630-5614-bb65-6a847dc17c14" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2bcf5c5-3054-552b-af30-86ba1b0dd0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375048} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1044e7-3d5d-535c-8f32-0ad68770cf94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375048} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de6ad9a-d1d9-5f41-ab7f-791941f541df" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19dbef9e-0809-539c-9b39-0f38e27bfee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375051} {:answer/id #uuid "644b08af-c3d3-5b7c-8896-41e6cf852e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375052} {:answer/id #uuid "49569de0-37bd-5498-bb31-a1a40f7a7d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375053} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeeff74-9b22-579d-ba7b-129b3580c48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375051} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b1e0a0-9d9e-5c07-b775-0547d0086c31" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45ffbed4-b922-5228-a76d-391a5a4a19a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343405" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (4{n}^{2}-1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\pi } & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c7ea26f-c465-5644-8f29-00fe94ff681b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1800471e-9188-5bb1-9aed-d377de1f651c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315257} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce831323-8c63-59e9-8d6f-c9bf55f74094" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66845167-3565-541d-84d8-7b2d1bbcac26" :question/math-academy-id "q-342496" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(2)=-{2}^{2}=-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "648c2889-db3b-5dfc-94a8-f9019704bcd0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481674}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481567}]}
